00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:02.340
Okay, guys, if you wanna learn more

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.090
about everything happening
here at Full Sail,

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.650
head to fullsail.edu or click
the link in the description,

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.330
and now, back to the show.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:10.530
Over the past few years,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.410
Netflix has solidified its move

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:15.640
from giant movie platform
to gianter movie maker,

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:16.960
adding plenty of popular

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.060
and profitable titles in its library.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.220
And they've got range:

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.050
from foreign language prestige pictures

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:23.970
like Ramin Bahrani's "The White Tiger,"

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.070
to action westerns like
"The Harder They Fall,"

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.300
to the vaguely disturbing "Gerald's Game."

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:29.540
At first glance, these films

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.400
don't seem to have all
that much in common.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:32.440
But dig a little deeper,

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.880
and you'll find plenty of
aesthetic similarities.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.060
Take the cinematography.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.220
It tends to feel very slick,
manufactured, and smooth.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.820
Even Bahrani, who previously
made a name for himself

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.830
directing gritty, realistic dramas,

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.800
essentially subsumed his
style into this cleaner look.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.810
Netflix films also tend to
share a similar color scheme,

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:51.760
with deeply-saturated hues

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.520
that render the actors
practically phosphorescent,

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.330
and make the slums of
India or a Wild West saloon

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.270
look almost as lush as the
mansions in "He's All That."

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:03.080
At the same time, the images
are relatively low-contrast,

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.930
making them easy, even
pleasant, on the eyes.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.380
Even the use of focus
tends to be pretty uniform,

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:10.670
often evoking a glamorous softness

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:12.140
that would have been standard fare

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.640
for, say, a 1940s melodrama.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.130
So what is this look really amounting to?

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:19.140
Let's examine the sum of the parts:

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.170
slick, functional cinematography;

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:22.690
low-contrast lighting;

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.390
saturated, matte colors;

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.940
and simple, no-nonsense compositions.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.160
This all makes for an easy, comfortable,

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.080
non-confrontational aesthetic.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:31.930
Which is to say,

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:34.310
it's basically ideal
for passive consumption

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:36.810
while folding your
freshly-laundered collection

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:38.340
of Wisecrack T-shirts.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.560
The passivity these films mostly engender

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.060
seem to be part of the point.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.747
As Matt Singer writes for ScreenCrush,

00:03:43.747 --> 00:03:46.520
"It's not that these Netflix
movies can't be enjoyed

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:48.910
while you are distracted by
your phone or your tablet;

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:51.640
it's that they are
undeniably better that way."

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:53.620
Of course, there are exceptions.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:56.460
For example, prestige films
made by big-name directors

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.760
like Martin Scorsese's "The Irishman"

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:00.580
or Alfonso Cuaron's "Roma."

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:01.600
But, more than anything,

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.280
these exceptions only make
the overall house style

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.200
of the platform stand out even more.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:07.730
Compare the richness of the colors

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:09.570
in "The Irishman's" film photography,

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.840
or the expansive scope of
"Roma's" wide-screen framing

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.260
to your average Netflix original,

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.820
and you'll see just how
different these movies are

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:17.830
from the norm.

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:20.800
And the Netflix norm when it
comes to style of filmmaking

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.000
is actually ideal for how
the site itself is built.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.970
Namely, the fact that Netflix hinges

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.830
on a very specific type
of cinematic experience:

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.420
one that never ends.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.110
How many times have you just finished

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:33.720
watching a great movie on Netflix,

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.610
soaking it all in as the credits roll,

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:36.770
only to be snapped out of it

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:39.560
the moment the trailer
for "Tall Girl" pops up?

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.010
Obviously, Netflix wants people to watch

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:43.470
as many of their films as possible,

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:46.670
so auto-playing a new
flick ASAP helps with that.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:48.240
This all leads into the effects

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.650
of Netflix's algorithmization
of its films.

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:53.730
You already know about Netflix's
famous embedded algorithm

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:56.440
that tracks the content you
watch, among other things,

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.930
so that it can recommend
other movies and shows

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:00.600
you're also likely to enjoy.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:01.760
And what's an easy,

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.310
and almost subconscious,
way of convincing you

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:06.220
that you'll enjoy the next recommendation?

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:07.400
If, at first glance,

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.870
this new film looks like
the last thing you watched.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.340
And that's what makes this trend

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:14.430
towards homogenous aesthetics
such a valuable tool.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:17.350
Now, there is an argument
to be made that algorithms

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:20.060
can actually be more
democratic than curation.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:21.590
Instead of relying on critics

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:23.940
or that film school hipster
you went on a date with,

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.390
AI uses your own personal taste

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:28.470
to determine what movies
are worth your time.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.080
But while this sounds great in theory,

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:32.480
the actual result doesn't
always work out that way.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.830
In practice, algorithms often
skew rather conservatively.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:37.620
That is to say, they tend to rely

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.160
on a known quantity of
data they have about you,

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.730
rather than risk surprising
you with an outlier

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:44.540
that might not suit your taste.

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:46.240
In other words, if the platform detects

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.067
that you have previously enjoyed
films like "Mystic River,"

00:05:49.067 --> 00:05:51.080
"Se7en," and "The Shawshank Redemption,"

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:54.040
then the algorithm will take
a lot of factors into account

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:55.990
in order to cater to your tastes.

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:58.410
Which probably means they
won't suggest you watch,

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.840
say, "Paddington 2," which is a shame,

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.670
because that movie is a masterpiece.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:05.550
And when we say they'll take
a lot of factors into account,

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:06.830
we are not kidding.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:10.360
As Todd Yellin, Netflix's VP
of Product told WIRED magazine,

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:12.190
the algorithm is able to split users

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:14.500
into 2,000 different taste groups

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:16.850
based on the explicit and implicit data

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:18.270
that each user had shown.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:20.580
That includes both data
actively given by you,

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.770
like a thumbs up rating,
or passive data collected,

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:26.270
like your infinite scrolling
through Oscar-contenders,

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:28.440
or the time you binged
"Squid Game" in one week.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:31.150
Now, this system sounds
sophisticated enough,

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:34.660
but it can still easily fall
prey to algorithmic bias.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:37.090
It's pretty convenient
and generally pleasant

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:40.010
to receive more movie
recommendations that remind you of...

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:41.400
- [David Mills] What's in the box?

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.580
But it can also lead to a feedback loop.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:45.410
Scholar Dal Yong Jin argues

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:48.010
that the algorithm's goal
of serving up a new movie

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:49.210
that you're certain to like

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:50.950
functionally limits your opportunities

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:54.340
to explore films in a
variety of genres or themes.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:55.780
What's more, the algorithm

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:58.510
typically keeps viewers
culturally confined by,

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:01.600
for example, mostly
recommending American films

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:03.190
to American viewers.

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:04.930
What's the point of paying for access

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:06.640
to a gargantuan library

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:10.260
if you're functionally never
going to expand your horizons?

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.430
But when this starts feeling even more sus

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:14.890
is the fact that the
algorithmization of Netflix

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:17.290
extends far beyond your personal queue.

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:19.450
It can actually affect
the new works of art

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:20.950
that are being produced.

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:22.360
Way back in 2018,

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:26.210
former VP of original content
Cindy Holland told Vulture

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:28.100
that Netflix was greenlighting projects

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:30.767
based on 70% human analysis

00:07:30.767 --> 00:07:33.110
and 30% data from the algorithm.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:34.520
Data including specifics

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:36.670
like how long you watched a given film,

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:39.350
whether you stopped watching
because it was too scary,

00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:40.210
and whether you stuck around

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:41.790
to start yet another scary movie

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:44.610
only to step away like a little coward...

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:46.350
or maybe that's just me.

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.680
Anyway, we suspect that
data has been given

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:51.820
an even more privileged position
at Netflix in recent years,

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:53.760
but call that speculation.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.500
Now, it's one thing when film tropes

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.770
evolve naturally through popular culture.

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:00.300
But when the computer
starts playing storyteller,

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.440
we get a little shiver down our spine.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:04.840
It's not that it's dystopian.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.360
I mean, it might be,
but it's also often bad.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:09.970
Take the new Ryan Reynolds
film "The Adam Project,"

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:12.100
which Vulture perfectly described as

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:13.830
feeling like a fake movie,

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:16.600
designed to check as many
genre boxes as possible

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:18.950
to maximize its chance of success.

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:20.270
But critical misfires

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:22.460
like superhero-time-traveling-Ryan
Reynolds

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:23.910
encountering his childhood self

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:25.830
can't stop Netflix's algorithm,

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:29.050
estimated to be worth a cool $1 billion.

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:30.610
It's clearly seen as valuable

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:33.350
for its ability to predict
what people will want to watch

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:35.490
based on what they've previously enjoyed.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:36.800
And, to state the obvious,

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:39.630
this isn't entirely unique in
the entertainment industry.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.710
Studios have always used
past successes and failures

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:44.180
when deciding what to make next,

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:46.330
or when predicting box-office hits.

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:49.710
But obviously, Netflix has
the great gift of big data

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:53.020
to make those decisions more
approximate, more scientific,

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:54.590
and ultimately, less risky.

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:56.440
Which is why some of the rest of Hollywood

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:59.050
has been taking note and
starting to consult AI

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:00.800
during the film development phase.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:02.410
What's more, Netflix arguably

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:04.730
has more sway and
influence over the industry

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:06.870
than just about any other company.

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:08.840
That's perhaps why we see its aesthetic

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:11.130
making its way into the industry at large.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:12.850
So, what does it mean when Netflix

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:15.930
makes its content decisions
in tandem with an algorithm

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:18.980
that's predicated on your
previous cinematic taste?

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:21.580
Well, when you're more worried
about holding attention

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:23.040
and keeping butts on couches

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:24.780
than you are about making good art,

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:28.250
you arguably lose much of
what makes cinema so important

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:30.270
in the first place: the risk factor.

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:33.490
See, most films that are truly
provocative and compelling

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:36.400
probably wouldn't make it through
the old Netflix algorithm.

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:38.870
The reason cult classics
are usually delightful

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:40.146
is their sheer shock value.

00:09:40.146 --> 00:09:42.560
(everyone screaming)

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:45.640
Would Netflix greenlight a
film starring an unknown actor,

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:47.760
about a curly-haired weirdo in Idaho

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.220
who plays tetherball all day?

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:51.180
Heck, would a retelling of the "Odyssey"

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:54.700
set in Depression-era America
sound sexy to an algorithm?

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:56.970
And how would something
as weird as "Tree of Life"

00:09:56.970 --> 00:10:00.050
ever possibly make it past
Netflix's head of content?

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:02.910
Now, it's no surprise that
in our TikTok-happy culture,

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:04.530
commercial filmmaking in general

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:05.940
has become more preoccupied

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:07.710
with keeping you from looking away

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:09.480
via high-voltage special effects,

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.520
laser-fast editing cuts, and so on.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:14.060
But we think algorithms
take it one step farther

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:16.330
when they assume that
we're only down to watch

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:19.060
what we've already
watched, but regurgitated.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:21.240
Of course, there's a
decent argument to be made

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:22.710
that much of cinematic history

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:24.700
has been somewhat self-cannibalizing.

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:26.590
And you don't have to
look at four generations

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:28.280
of "A Star is Born" for evidence.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:31.240
The assembly-line logic that
fueled Hollywood's golden era

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:33.840
often meant rearranging
your contract actors

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.290
in various trope-ish roles
like strong silent type,

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:39.190
or the manipulative vamp,

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:41.340
or the plucky young innocents.

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.540
These characters would
then, almost inevitably,

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:46.510
go through storylines of
lost love, love regained,

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:49.360
and hey, maybe there's a peppy
musical number in there, too.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:51.380
Still, Hollywood nonetheless exhibited

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:53.067
what scholar Janet Staiger called,

00:10:53.067 --> 00:10:55.440
"A drive towards product differentiation,

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:57.230
stressing novelty, and innovation

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:01.110
in order to lure audiences to
the cinema again and again."

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:04.220
They needed people to be
actively excited to watch.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:05.930
Netflix doesn't have the same burden.

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:09.030
It's often the background
soundtrack to your domestic life.

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:11.290
And arguably what keeps
you from turning it off

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:13.670
is familiarity or sameness.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:15.810
That is, entertainment that reminds you

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:18.000
of previously-enjoyable entertainment,

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:21.160
or as we like to call it: algortainment.

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:22.310
Is that not catchy?

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:23.460
Is it not?

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:24.920
Well, okay, I'll never say it again.

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:25.870
We can understand this

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:28.470
via the philosophical
movement of Postmodernism,

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:30.120
and its application to aesthetics,

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:32.910
which has been percolating
in academia since the '70s.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:34.430
Postmodernism is predicated

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:37.360
on the notion that society
has become so fragmented

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:39.640
that there's been a
breakdown in subjectivity,

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:44.030
i.e., there is no longer any
consistent and universal truth

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:47.430
that grounds how we view
art or our own subjectivity.

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:49.330
If we used to all have a loose agreement

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:51.740
on what counted as art, or cinema,

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:53.050
we're now in a free-for-all,

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:55.890
where a TikTok is as
valid as a Scorsese film.

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:58.300
This comes with challenges.

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.517
As literary critic
Fredric Jameson explains,

00:12:00.517 --> 00:12:03.320
"If, indeed, the subject
has lost its capacity

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:06.480
to organize its past and future
into coherent experience,

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:07.930
it becomes difficult enough

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:09.710
to see how the cultural productions

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:11.920
of such a subject could result in anything

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:13.570
but heaps of fragments."

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:15.280
In other words, when it no longer feels

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:17.240
like there are universal
truths and concepts

00:12:17.240 --> 00:12:20.140
that help you make consistent
sense of experience over time,

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:23.190
it's harder to turn your
unique experiences into art.

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:25.020
And it's even harder for a bunch of folks

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:26.760
with disparate experiences
and perspectives

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:30.110
to all agree on what art
even is in the first place,

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:32.930
let alone, agree on what
art is actually good.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:35.440
So, where do we get the raw material

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:37.630
to create those heaps of fragments?

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:39.900
We start pillaging dead
artists, of course!

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:41.797
As Jameson puts it, in the postmodern era,

00:12:41.797 --> 00:12:44.570
"The producers of culture
have nowhere to turn

00:12:44.570 --> 00:12:47.180
but to the past: the
imitation of dead styles,

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:48.740
speech through all the masks

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:51.260
and voices stored up
in the imaginary museum

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:52.880
of a now global culture."

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:54.336
He calls the practice of invoking

00:12:54.336 --> 00:12:57.720
or straight-up reviving old
styles of art, pastiche,

00:12:57.720 --> 00:12:59.850
and considers it a fundamental tool

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:01.440
of making postmodern art.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:04.650
Of course, pastiche isn't
necessarily a bad thing.

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:07.180
Without pastiche, you
wouldn't have "Pulp Fiction,"

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:08.840
or the novels of Thomas Pynchon,

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:11.470
or the sparkly '60s camp of "Hairspray."

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:13.550
The postmodern turn reveled in the idea

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:16.060
that no piece of art is purely creative,

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:18.760
that we're all cross-inspired
by other artists:

00:13:18.760 --> 00:13:20.190
past, present and future.

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:22.740
And postmodern artists
used various techniques

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:26.200
of ripping each other off to
make truly groundbreaking work

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:28.030
as wide ranging as "Blue Velvet"

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:30.050
and "Monty Python and the Holy Grail."

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:33.020
But not all works of postmodern
art can be brilliant.

00:13:33.020 --> 00:13:35.250
Jameson acknowledged that postmodernism

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:38.290
necessarily implied a
sort of cultural stasis.

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:39.227
And in that stasis,

00:13:39.227 --> 00:13:42.240
"The supreme value of
the new and of innovation

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.210
fades away against the steady stream

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:45.700
of momentum and variation

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:48.960
that at some outer limit
seems stable and motionless.

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:51.250
Where everything now submits
to the perpetual change

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:52.770
of fashion and media image,

00:13:52.770 --> 00:13:54.530
nothing can change any longer."

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:56.270
So you get a lot of sameness,

00:13:56.270 --> 00:13:58.960
and a lack of new
coherent artistic styles.

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:01.097
Simultaneously, as Jameson explains,

00:14:01.097 --> 00:14:03.960
"What has happened is that
aesthetic production today

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:07.130
has become integrated into
commodity production generally,"

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:09.640
and now faces "The
frantic economic urgency

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:13.150
of producing fresh waves of
ever-more-novel-seeming goods

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:15.140
at ever greater rates of turnover."

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:17.010
In this way, whereas art objects

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:19.230
used to have value
relative to their beauty

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:20.360
or aesthetic style,

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:24.090
they are now simply commodities
to be produced and sold.

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:25.500
It's not shocking that Jameson

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:27.087
once defined postmodernism as,

00:14:27.087 --> 00:14:29.610
"The cultural logic of late capitalism."

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:32.210
So, just as originality
has slumped to a crawl,

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:35.070
demand for production has skyrocketed.

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:36.800
And who does that remind us of?

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:39.120
Our good red-enveloped friend, of course.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:42.040
Now, to be clear, Netflix
hasn't dedicated itself

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:43.550
to the postmodern project

00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:45.770
the way that the cool
kids at art school have.

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:48.370
They're just trying to get
more eyes on their movies

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:49.980
for longer periods of time.

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:51.670
To be clear, we're not saying

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:53.430
all movies used to be masterpieces.

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:55.410
Some cinema used to try to be art,

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:58.290
while some was content
being popcorn blockbusters.

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:00.110
And Netflix has tried both strategies

00:15:00.110 --> 00:15:03.010
to amass its giant
portfolio of original work,

00:15:03.010 --> 00:15:05.630
all in attempts to
achieve its supreme goal

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:07.440
of maximizing watch time.

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:09.080
It's not even up to us to say

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:10.730
if that came at the expense of quality,

00:15:10.730 --> 00:15:13.300
because Netflix's COO Ted Sarandos

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:14.920
has implied as much himself.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:17.450
In response to critics panning
Netflix's film "Bright,"

00:15:17.450 --> 00:15:18.730
Sarandos defended the film,

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:21.210
saying that critics, "Speak
to specific audiences

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:22.530
who care about quality,

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:26.330
or how objectively good or bad
a movie is, not the masses."

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:27.450
Implying, of course,

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:30.080
that the masses don't care
about objective quality,

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:32.940
and that Netflix is okay
not actually delivering it.

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.940
This reminds us of philosopher
Theodor Adorno's critique

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:38.140
of mid-20th century popular music.

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:40.310
As scholar C.W. Smith explains,

00:15:40.310 --> 00:15:43.250
Adorno believed that people
were listening to popular music

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:44.660
without actually listening

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:46.617
because they were
experiencing what he called,

00:15:46.617 --> 00:15:49.210
"A breakdown in mediating subjectivity

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:51.420
or the intersubjectivity of the experience

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:53.530
between the subject and
the piece of music."

00:15:53.530 --> 00:15:55.900
That is to say, people were so inundated

00:15:55.900 --> 00:15:57.140
with homogenous pop music

00:15:57.140 --> 00:15:59.230
that they started consuming it mindlessly,

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:02.460
rather than engaging with
any single song thoughtfully.

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:04.430
For Adorno, this was about more

00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:05.910
than just spacing out, though.

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:07.160
He contended that this sort of

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:09.010
undiscerning consumption of art

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:12.150
indicated a broader social
tendency towards obedience,

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:14.160
even an acceptance of authoritarianism,

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:16.480
which can prevent us from
becoming free-thinking,

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:17.750
reflective individuals.

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:19.860
That is to say, when we
stop thinking critically

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:23.240
about the art we consume,
we become less autonomous

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:28.000
and instead more pliable,
suggestible, and easier to coerce.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:31.240
The interminable sameness
Adorno observed, he believed,

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:34.050
led to both a, "Regression
in quality of hearing,"

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:36.640
and "The more or less
stunting of experience,

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:38.800
such that the listener was docile

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:40.300
and afraid of anything new."

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:42.470
And that sense of safety in familiarity

00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:45.510
sounds pretty familiar when
we think about Netflix.

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:47.610
As we've discussed, Netflix films are made

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:50.740
for the sort of watching-non-watching
Adorno diagnosed

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:52.330
in mid-century music listeners.

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:53.620
And there's reason to believe viewers

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:56.040
might indeed be afraid of anything new.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:58.750
Stephen Follows, founder
of VOD Clickstream,

00:16:58.750 --> 00:17:00.100
which analyzes Netflix data,

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:02.817
argued in a podcast that
when people watch Netflix,

00:17:02.817 --> 00:17:03.710
"They're sitting down

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:05.590
and watching stuff that is comfortable,

00:17:05.590 --> 00:17:08.690
that is easy to understand,
that won't challenge them."

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:10.320
The appeal of the algorithm

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:13.600
is its infinitely watchable
lack of confrontation.

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:15.240
And look, we're not monsters,

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:16.910
we love us some easy-watching

00:17:16.910 --> 00:17:18.900
as much as we love Frank Sinatra.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:21.120
Lord knows, I've watched
the episode of "The Office"

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:24.017
where Dwight quotes
Mussolini about 76 times.

00:17:24.017 --> 00:17:25.670
(podium thudding)

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.400
- Blood alone moves the wheels of history!

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.200
- But does constant easy-watching

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:33.100
come at the expense of our
ability to really watch?

00:17:33.100 --> 00:17:34.350
And when arguably the world's

00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:37.010
most powerful media
corporation commits itself

00:17:37.010 --> 00:17:40.120
to making movies we're
all-but-guaranteed to half-watch,

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:42.890
does it change the way we
engage with the medium overall?

00:17:42.890 --> 00:17:44.940
And when we consider the algorithmization

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:47.100
of most aspects of our modern lives,

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:49.530
the problem starts to feel way scarier.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:52.120
But hey, at least we get
to watch "Is it Cake?"

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:53.220
But what do you guys think?

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:54.680
When it comes to Netflix films,

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:57.000
are we actually getting
the cinema we want,

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.670
or merely the cinema the
algorithm thinks we want?

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:01.990
What's more, are we inadvertently creating

00:18:01.990 --> 00:18:04.130
our own cinematic hell when we hate-watch

00:18:04.130 --> 00:18:06.130
some of Netflix's more mediocre content?

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:07.600
Let us know in the comments.

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:10.000
Big thanks to our patrons
for all your support.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.000
Please go check out our
revamped Patreon page!

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:13.880
We have lots of good stuff in there.

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:15.010
Hit that subscribe button

00:18:15.010 --> 00:18:17.670
like you're jumping up to
give the tall girl a high-five

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:19.350
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:21.745
And as always, thanks for watching. Later.

00:18:21.745 --> 00:18:25.245
(upbeat electronic music)

